    "narrative": "The Ghost Ride the WHIP lineup is headlined by **Corbin Carroll** (80.77) and **Vinnie Pasquantino** (76.21), our two most bankable assets today. **Corbin Carroll** remains an elite start against Andrew Abbott, where the favorable xERA of 5.01 and a park-factor boost outweigh the inherent platoon disadvantage of a lefty-lefty matchup. **Vinnie Pasquantino** serves as our primary offensive anchor; he draws a highly favorable matchup against Jacob Waguespack, where his ability to exploit a right-handed starter provides a +14% platoon efficiency jump that cements his high score. **Gabriel Moreno** (66.77) and **Brice Turang** (62.16) round out our top-tier plays, both benefiting from high-leverage lineup slots and shaky opposing pitching. Notably, **Henry Bolte** (60.79) earns a spot start, as his projected lead-off position provides the volume necessary to remain relevant despite a difficult assignment against Javier.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision involved keeping **Seiya Suzuki** (48.51) on the bench. While **Seiya Suzuki** posted a respectable score, he fell just short of the efficiency threshold when compared to the ceiling of **Zach McKinstry** (52.96), whose platoon edge against the lefty Lynch IV provided the necessary mathematical separation. We continue to monitor the status of **Max Muncy** (51.96), **Travis Bazzana** (57.55), and **Griffin Conine** (51.53), as their status as \"Lineup Pending\" introduces volatility to their production floors; should **Max Muncy** slide further down the order, his utility score is at risk of falling below our 40-point maintenance floor.\n\nRisk mitigation is paramount today, particularly with **Austin Riley** (48.83) and **Josh Naylor** (50.14) facing strong arms in Shane Drohan and Shota Imanaga, respectively. **Austin Riley** is currently starting despite a suppressed score due to a lack of immediate internal alternatives, but he remains a candidate for mid-game substitution if his efficiency dips. We are banking on the high-contact environment for **Mickey Moniak** (57.73), whose park boost against Foster Griffin is significant, though we must remain cautious of his slow-delivery defensive metrics. We will track the final lineups closely to ensure the projected #1\u2013#3 hitters for **Gabriel Moreno**, **Brice Turang**, and **Corbin Carroll** hold, as their batting order premiums are currently baked into their strong efficiency scores.",
